aps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,5\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}})}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}\\cdot (216-125) \\\\  & =\\frac{182\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b62b53-1b76-5884-8cf6-e623e6a2f279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539394} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6d3790-da14-581b-85b9-f63924341cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539400} {:answer/id #uuid "49ad23b9-5753-5821-bf10-fcf46d63b245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539414} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0087ee-6487-5333-81b9-0c1398c381ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539416} {:answer/id #uuid "8e62ca35-4a8a-52bf-a4f8-7c4ea42efb76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d6d3790-da14-581b-85b9-f63924341cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539400} :answer-field/id #uuid "b807e3c0-f519-5770-bffe-d3aeea20ea64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a0728d4-a0d3-515f-805e-eb1a7d3c019c" :question/problem "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $25\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+25t-20t=5(12+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12.5-\\frac{4Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{12+t}\\,Q=12.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(12+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{4}\\cdot 12.5 \\\\ (12+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(12+t)^{3}Q & =12.5(12+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{4}) & =12.5(12+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 12.5(12+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{4} & =12.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(12+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2.5(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 2.5(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{4}} & =10 \\\\ 30+\\frac{C}{(12)^{4}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{4}} & =-20 \\\\ C & =-20(12)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2.5(12+t)-\\frac{20(12)^{4}}{(12+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =2.5(12+t)-20{(\\frac{12}{12+t})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 60+25{t}_{o}-20{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 60+5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24) & =2.5(12+24)-20{(\\frac{12}{12+24})}^{4} \\\\  & =90-20{(\\frac{12}{36})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈89.8\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3544b068-279b-57e6-9f38-0c9eaa23d33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "235.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539406} {:answer/id #uuid "69907d51-3bf1-5b65-bbb2-ae831146ccb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "222.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539422} {:answer/id #uuid "872c8957-d06f-5407-86a5-616dbaa82761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "117.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539430} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fcf795-dfb5-51ba-9ea4-76d781abc0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "246.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539450} {:answer/id #uuid "e82e736f-6d11-5a2d-83f2-a27bd4f7db0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3544b068-279b-57e6-9f38-0c9eaa23d33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "235.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539406} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f10b853-d565-5f3f-8bc1-e376bfffca18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21bb65fc-94da-5d56-9b2a-2fe8f762fdb9" :question/problem "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $368\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $160\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =16\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=300+20t-12t=8(37.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5Q}{37.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =16-\\frac{1.5Q}{37.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{37.5+t}\\,Q=16.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1.5/(37.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|37.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(37.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =(37.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(37.5+t)^{1.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{37.5+t}\\,Q) & =(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot 16 \\\\ (37.5+t)^{1.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+1.5(37.5+t)^{0.5}Q & =16(37.5+t)^{1.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(37.5+t)^{1.5}) & =16(37.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(37.5+t)^{1.5})\\,dt & =\\int 16(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(37.5+t)^{1.5} & =16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2.5}(37.5+t)^{2.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6.4(37.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(37.5+t)^{1.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $160\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=160,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =160 \\\\ 6.4(37.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(37.5+0)^{1.5}} & =160 \\\\ 240+\\frac{C}{(37.5)^{1.5}} & =160 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{37.5}^{1.5}} & =-80 \\\\ C & =-80(37.5)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6.4(37.5+t)-\\frac{80(37.5)^{1.5}}{(37.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =6.4(37.5+t)-80{(\\frac{37.5}{37.5+t})}^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $368\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=368$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =368 \\\\ 300+20{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =368 \\\\ 300+8{t}_{o} & =368 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =68 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =8.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8.5) & =6.4(37.5+8.5)-80{(\\frac{37.5}{37.5+8.5})}^{1.5} \\\\  & =294.4-80{(\\frac{37.5}{46})}^{1.5} \\\\  & ≈235.5\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa09e5-f47d-5621-9398-dc8978b0d87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "316.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539393} {:answer/id #uuid "3c471766-6b4e-5806-89be-2166ca930256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "328.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539409} {:answer/id #uuid "89c88c07-7e92-5645-a2bb-e6beb66e7182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "301.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539431} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a76597-8ded-5bde-a591-93a2815617ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "287.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539443} {:answer/id #uuid "d57c8381-a0fe-5352-bcb4-fdc5674d137c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "265.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89c88c07-7e92-5645-a2bb-e6beb66e7182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "301.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539431} :answer-field/id #uuid "b68e3566-38f2-5496-a173-8df05cbc7a77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a39de0b-a8fb-57f4-97a8-3f35b38b481a" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $492\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $213\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $\\frac{142}{3}\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =10\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $213\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=213+15t-9t=6(35.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5Q}{35.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =10-\\frac{1.5Q}{35.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{35.5+t}\\,Q=10.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1.5/(35.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|35.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(35.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =(35.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(35.5+t)^{1.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(35.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{35.5+t}\\,Q) & =(35.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot 10 \\\\ (35.5+t)^{1.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+1.5(35.5+t)^{0.5}Q & =10(35.5+t)^{1.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(35.5+t)^{1.5}) & =10(35.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(35.5+t)^{1.5})\\,dt & =\\int 10(35.5+t)^{1.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(35.5+t)^{1.5} & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2.5}(35.5+t)^{2.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(35.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(35.5+t)^{1.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $\\frac{142}{3}\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=\\frac{142}{3},$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =\\frac{142}{3} \\\\ 4(35.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(35.5+0)^{1.5}} & =\\frac{142}{3} \\\\ 142+\\frac{C}{(35.5)^{1.5}} & =\\frac{142}{3} \\\\ \\frac{C}{(35.5)^{1.5}} & =-\\frac{284}{3} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{284}{3}(35.5)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(35.5+t)-\\frac{\\frac{284}{3}(35.5)^{1.5}}{(35.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =4(35.5+t)-\\frac{284}{3}{(\\frac{35.5}{35.5+t})}^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $492\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=492$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =492 \\\\ 213+15{t}_{o}-9{t}_{o} & =492 \\\\ 213+6{t}_{o} & =492 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =279 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =46.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(46.5) & =4(35.5+46.5)-\\frac{284}{3}{(\\frac{35.5}{35.5+46.5})}^{1.5} \\\\  & =328-\\frac{284}{3}{(\\frac{35.5}{82})}^{1.5} \\\\  & ≈301.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b4b74e-a664-52ce-a195-21a3d605fb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539411} {:answer/id #uuid "71401fcb-b5f8-5b4c-8bf5-f003372af32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539424} {:answer/id #uuid "989c22ac-092f-5f6b-a7b9-e236e2ba3d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539434} {:answer/id #uuid "a796dcf6-c12a-5697-9493-d0728388bc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539437} {:answer/id #uuid "cf523ecc-0427-56f9-a3d5-e87e43240325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf523ecc-0427-56f9-a3d5-e87e43240325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539447} :answer-field/id #uuid "22f5cfc2-660c-58a7-af4f-432fa13dacdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6991e06d-da9f-57bf-8c9b-3cc0e133e514" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $80\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $32\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $4\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $32\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=32+8t-6t=2(16+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{16+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{3Q}{16+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{16+t}\\,Q=4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(16+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|16+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =(16+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(16+t)^{3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(16+t)^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{16+t}\\,Q) & =(16+t)^{3}\\cdot 4 \\\\ (16+t)^{3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(16+t)^{2}Q & =4(16+t)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16+t)^{3}) & =4(16+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16+t)^{3})\\,dt & =\\int 4(16+t)^{3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(16+t)^{3} & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(16+t)^{4}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1(16+t)+\\frac{C}{(16+t)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $4\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=4,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =4 \\\\ 1(16+0)+\\frac{C}{(16+0)^{3}} & =4 \\\\ 16+\\frac{C}{(16)^{3}} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(16)^{3}} & =-12 \\\\ C & =-12(16)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1(16+t)-\\frac{12(16)^{3}}{(16+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =1(16+t)-12{(\\frac{16}{16+t})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $80\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=80$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =80 \\\\ 32+8{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =80 \\\\ 32+2{t}_{o} & =80 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =48 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24) & =1(16+24)-12{(\\frac{16}{16+24})}^{3} \\\\  & =40-12{(\\frac{16}{40})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈39.2\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1accc569-7a13-58f0-bb4e-fe4d837666e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539399} {:answer/id #uuid "4c421d3c-a021-523f-b9ba-63f0c8410839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539415} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e8af6d-9653-5a1a-9535-1b2052824104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539440} {:answer/id #uuid "d8aad8df-e1a6-5036-96dc-1c9eea79d2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539449} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ac5ae2-38a9-5b58-b64a-b2b42879cc90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2e8af6d-9653-5a1a-9535-1b2052824104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539440} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03249a1-4a5a-5426-a9d8-a51d09a0bcc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7375207b-1d18-5bc8-88aa-4e960cad1491" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $82\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $33\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $\\frac{33}{8}\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $3\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $1\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $3\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (3\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (3\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $33\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $3\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $1\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=33+3t-1t=2(16.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $1\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(1\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(1\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.5Q}{16.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{0.5Q}{16.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{16.5+t}\\,Q=1.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 0.5/(16.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|16.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =(16.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(16.5+t)^{0.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{16.5+t}\\,Q) & =(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (16.5+t)^{0.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+0.5(16.5+t)^{-0.5}Q & =1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16.5+t)^{0.5}) & =1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16.5+t)^{0.5})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(16.5+t)^{0.5} & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1.5}(16.5+t)^{1.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1(16.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(16.5+t)^{0.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $\\frac{33}{8}\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=\\frac{33}{8},$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ 1(16.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(16.5+0)^{0.5}} & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ 16.5+\\frac{C}{(16.5)^{0.5}} & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ \\frac{C}{(16.5)^{0.5}} & =-\\frac{99}{8} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{99}{8}(16.5)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1(16.5+t)-\\frac{\\frac{99}{8}(16.5)^{0.5}}{(16.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =1(16.5+t)-\\frac{99}{8}{(\\frac{16.5}{16.5+t})}^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $82\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=82$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =82 \\\\ 33+3{t}_{o}-1{t}_{o} & =82 \\\\ 33+2{t}_{o} & =82 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =49 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24.5) & =1(16.5+24.5)-\\frac{99}{8}{(\\frac{16.5}{16.5+24.5})}^{0.5} \\\\  & =41-\\frac{99}{8}{(\\frac{16.5}{41})}^{0.5} \\\\  & ≈33.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19329e8b-f3ba-516f-af71-d4b1d311b395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "233.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539396} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebc62f3-c951-51bd-a0c0-b13a63587c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "193.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539410} {:answer/id #uuid "8e496fc1-51c0-50fa-b05e-7b8545cfbf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539432} {:answer/id #uuid "ec958c6a-8b57-50b9-9e6f-b329672750e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539455} {:answer/id #uuid "fed2300e-a029-550d-98c0-c43563de994e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "209.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec958c6a-8b57-50b9-9e6f-b329672750e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539455} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a6811d-be8c-5fac-8293-80fb147bef39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7b8493b-92bf-5e7a-80a8-38bdcc3f94e7" :question/problem "A nutrient tank for a hydroponic greenhouse can hold up to $608gal$ of solution. Initially, it contains $276gal$ of nutrient solution with $36.8lb$ of dissolved nutrient. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.4lb/gal$ flows into the tank at a rate of $20gal/min$, while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12gal/min$. Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.4\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $276\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=276+20t-12t=8(34.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5Q}{34.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{1.5Q}{34.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{34.5+t}\\,Q=8.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1.5/(34.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|34.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(34.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =(34.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(34.5+t)^{1.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(34.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{34.5+t}\\,Q) & =(34.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot 8 \\\\ (34.5+t)^{1.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+1.5(34.5+t)^{0.5}Q & =8(34.5+t)^{1.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(34.5+t)^{1.5}) & =8(34.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(34.5+t)^{1.5})\\,dt & =\\int 8(34.5+t)^{1.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(34.5+t)^{1.5} & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2.5}(34.5+t)^{2.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3.2(34.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(34.5+t)^{1.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $36.8\\,\\text{lb}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=36.8,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =36.8 \\\\ 3.2(34.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(34.5+0)^{1.5}} & =36.8 \\\\ 110.4+\\frac{C}{(34.5)^{1.5}} & =36.8 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(34.5)^{1.5}} & =-73.6 \\\\ C & =-73.6(34.5)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3.2(34.5+t)-\\frac{73.6(34.5)^{1.5}}{(34.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =3.2(34.5+t)-73.6{(\\frac{34.5}{34.5+t})}^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $608\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=608$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =608 \\\\ 276+20{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =608 \\\\ 276+8{t}_{o} & =608 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =332 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =41.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(41.5) & =3.2(34.5+41.5)-73.6{(\\frac{34.5}{34.5+41.5})}^{1.5} \\\\  & =243.2-73.6{(\\frac{34.5}{76})}^{1.5} \\\\  & ≈220.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
